# ===========================================================================
# SDC (Skilleddesk Code) — repository ignore rules
#
# This file sits at the git root and is authoritative for the whole repository.
# sdc/.gitignore adds the subproject's own generated paths on top of it.
#
# The order is deliberate: secrets first, because a mistake in that section costs
# a leaked credential rather than a dirty working tree. Everything below it is
# regenerable build output, then editor/OS noise, then local scratch.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Secrets and credentials — never commit, never `git add -f`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Local environment files. .env.example is the exception: it documents the shape
# and holds no values, which is the whole point of it existing.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
!.env.example

# Keys and certificates
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.ppk

# SSH material, in case a key is ever generated inside the tree
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
.ssh/

# Cloud / CI credential files that tooling drops in a working directory
.aws/
.gcloud/
.azure/
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
kubeconfig
.kube/

# Token stores. .npmrc is ignored because it is the usual place a registry token
# ends up; if this project ever needs a committed one, force-add a token-free
# file deliberately (`git add -f .npmrc`) and review it in the diff.
.npmrc
.yarnrc
.yarnrc.yml
.pypirc
.netrc
.secrets
.secrets.*
*.secret
*credentials*.json
*service-account*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Local databases — they hold chat history and tokens (spec §3.4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite-journal

# Encrypted backup archives (spec §9.11)
*.age
*.tar.age

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Dependencies and build output — regenerable, and huge
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Frontend bundle
dist/
dist-ssr/
.vite/

# Rust / Tauri build output (cargo target dirs, both crates)
target/
**/target/
**/src-tauri/gen/

# TypeScript incremental build info
*.tsbuildinfo

# Coverage and test output
coverage/
*.lcov
test-results/

# Logs
*.log
logs/
pnpm-debug.log*
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Editor and OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Personal editor state. .vscode/settings.json is deliberately NOT ignored: the
# repository ships it as shared project configuration.
.idea/
*.code-workspace

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Local scratch (this machine only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The verification harness used while building the UI: headless-browser driver
# scripts plus an Edge user-data profile. A browser profile is browsing state
# (cookies, history, autofill) and must never be published, so the whole
# directory is ignored rather than the profile inside it.
_verify/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. The owner's working documents - kept out of the published repository
#
# The three planning files at the repository root are ignored on purpose. They are
# the owner's own scratch: a product roadmap, a byte-identical copy of the design
# prototype, and a working note that logs this machine's setup (paths, versions,
# what was tried). Publishing a clone is not the same as publishing those.
#
# They stay on disk. To include them in a commit after all, remove the leading '#'
# from the three lines below - but do it deliberately, and remember that ignoring
# a file that is already committed does not remove it from history:
#
#   git log --all --full-history -- "Full Plan.txt" UI.txt NOTES.md
#
# The specification a contributor needs is sdc/docs/MASTER_SPEC.md, which *is*
# committed; nothing in the three files above is required to build or run SDC.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Full\ Plan.txt
UI.txt
NOTES.md

# Release artefacts built on this machine (installers, portable zip). They are
# products of the source tree, not part of it.
release/
